==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|hant||Scotland}}
* {{alt|en|ha'nt|haint|harnt||US|eye dialect}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:haunten<ety:bor<xno:hanter<ety:bor<ang:hāmettan>>>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|haunten||to reside, inhabit, use, employ}}, from {{der|en|fro|hanter||to inhabit, frequent, resort to}}, from {{der|en|fro-nor|hanter|t=to go back home, frequent}}, from {{der|en|non|heimta||to bring home, fetch}} or/and from {{der|en|ang|hāmettan||to bring home; house; cohabit with}}; both from {{der|en|gem-pro|*haimatjaną||to house, bring home}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*haimaz||village, home}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱóymos||village}}.

Cognate with {{cog|ang|hǣman|t=to cohabit, lie with, marry}}; related to {{cog|ang|hām||home, village}}, {{cog|fro|hantin||a stay, a place frequented by}} from the same Germanic source. Another descendant from the French is {{cog|nl|hanteren}}, whence {{cog|de|hantieren}}, {{cog|sv|hantera}}, {{cog|da|håndtere}}. More at [[home]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/hɔːnt/|a=RP}}, {{enPR|hônt}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-haunt.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/hɔnt/|a=US}}, {{enPR|hônt}}
** {{IPA|en|/hɑnt/|a=cot-caught}}, {{enPR|hänt}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔːnt|s1=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[inhabit]] or to [[visit]] [[frequently]] (most often used in reference to ghosts).
#: {{ux|en|A couple of ghosts '''haunt''' the old, burnt-down house.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|III|iv|52|1|passage=You wrong me Sir,&ZeroWidthSpace;thus ſtill to '''haunt''' my houſe.}}
#* {{RQ:Tasso Fairfax Godfrey of Bulloigne
|passage=Foul spirits '''haunt''' my resting place.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1713|author=w:Jonathan Swift|title=Imitation of Horace|section=Book I. Ep. VII
|passage=those cares that '''haunt''' the court and town}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make [[uneasy]], [[restless]].
#: {{ux|en|The memory of his past failures '''haunted''' him.}}
#* {{RQ:Tolkien Fellowship of the Ring|passage=The murder of Déagol '''haunted''' Gollum, and he had made up a defence.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[stalk]]; to [[follow]].
#: {{ux|en|The policeman '''haunted''' him, following him everywhere.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=w:Elle King; {{w|Dave Bassett (songwriter)|Dave Bassett}}|authorlabel=no|title=w:Ex's & Oh's|album=w:Love Stuff|date=23 September 2014|artist=Elle King|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0uLI6BnVh6w|text=Ex's and the oh-oh-oh's, they '''haunt''' me / Like ghosts, they want me / To make 'em a-a-all / They won't let go / Ex's and oh's}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|now|rare}} To [[live]] habitually; to stay, to [[remain]].
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|John|11|54|cxxxviij|verso=yes|Ieſus therfore walked no more openly amõge the iewes&nbsp;: butt went his waye thence vnto a countre ny to a wildernes into a cite called effraym&nbsp;/ and there '''haunted''' with his diſciples.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1590|III|X|stanza=40|554|{{...}}That yonder in that faithfull wilderneſſe&nbsp;/ Huge monſters '''haunt''',&ZeroWidthSpace;and many dangers dwell;{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|UK|_|dialectal|Northern England|Scotland}} To [[accustom]]; [[habituate]]; make accustomed to.
#* {{RQ:Wyclif Bible|termlang=en|I|1 Timothy|4|7||columns=1, 2|{{...}}haunte thi silf to pite [or ''pitee''].|{{...}}'''haunt''' thyself to piety.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|UK|_|dialectal|Northern England|Scotland}} To [[practise]]; to devote oneself to.
#* {{RQ:Ascham Scholemaster|passage=Leave honest pleasure, and '''haunt''' no good pastime.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To persist in staying or visiting.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|I|i|311|1|passage=I haue charg’d thee not to '''haunt''' about my doores:{{...}}}}

